  },
  {
    "id": "aquabliss-sf100",
    "name": "AquaBliss SF100 Multi-Stage Shower Filter",
    "badge": "Best Budget Multi-Stage",
    "category": "Inline Filter, Multi-Stage Media With Vitamin C",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "description": "AquaBliss is the bestselling budget inline filter, and it is a reasonable buy if you understand what it is. A retailer listing for its SFC100 cartridge describes a 12-stage mix including calcium sulfite, redox media, activated carbon, vitamin C, ceramic mineral beads and tourmaline, aimed at chlorine, sediment, organic impurities and odors in hot and cold water. AquaBliss's own guidance says to change the cartridge at least every six months, and a retailer estimates three to six months depending on your water. We did not find an NSF certification for it, so its performance claims are the brand's. The calcium sulfite and vitamin C are the stages doing the useful work in hot water; the mineral beads and tourmaline are marketing.",
    "pros": [
      "Low price",
      "Calcium sulfite and vitamin C stages suited to hot water",
      "Fits behind your existing showerhead",
      "Cartridges easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No NSF certification found",
      "Twelve stages is marketing more than engineering",
      "Cartridge life of three to six months"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A fine budget filter for chlorine taste and smell, as long as you treat its long list of stages as marketing and its claims as unverified."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Shower Head Filters",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-shower-head-filter/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best shower head filter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most city-water homes, the Aquasana AQ-4100 is our pick, because Aquasana says it was tested to NSF/ANSI 177 with over 90% chlorine reduction for 10,000 gallons or six months. For chloramine water, the Canopy filtered showerhead is the better choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do shower filters work for hard water?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not in any meaningful way. Hardness is dissolved calcium and magnesium, which shower filters are not designed to remove. A water softener is the fix for hard water, and some homes pair one with a shower filter for chlorine."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do shower filters remove chloramine?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some do, more slowly. Calcium sulfite and vitamin C are the media brands use for chloramine; KDF and ordinary carbon are commonly described as weak against it. Canopy, for example, lists calcium sulfite for chlorine and chloramine, while noting not every claim is independently tested."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should I change a shower filter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Follow the brand's interval. Our picks range from about 90 days for Jolie and Canopy to six months for Aquasana and Culligan and one year for the Sprite HO2, according to their makers and retailers. Heavier use or chloramine shortens cartridge life."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What does NSF/ANSI 177 mean?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is the standard for shower filtration systems, covering reduction of free available chlorine. Certified means an accredited body verified the claim; tested to means testing was done to the method, often commissioned by the brand. It does not cover claims about skin, hair or heavy metals."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will a shower filter lower my water pressure?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Slightly, in some cases. Filters add resistance, and many filtered showerheads use 1.8 gpm heads to meet state limits. A clogging cartridge also reduces flow, which is a sign to replace it or to look at sediment filtration for the whole house."
