        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best shower head filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most city-water homes, the Aquasana AQ-4100 is our pick, because Aquasana says it was tested to NSF/ANSI 177 with over 90% chlorine reduction for 10,000 gallons or six months. For chloramine water, the Canopy filtered showerhead is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do shower filters work for hard water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in any meaningful way. Hardness is dissolved calcium and magnesium, which shower filters are not designed to remove. A water softener is the fix for hard water, and some homes pair one with a shower filter for chlorine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do shower filters remove chloramine?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do, more slowly. Calcium sulfite and vitamin C are the media brands use for chloramine; KDF and ordinary carbon are commonly described as weak against it. Canopy, for example, lists calcium sulfite for chlorine and chloramine, while noting not every claim is independently tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change a shower filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the brand's interval. Our picks range from about 90 days for Jolie and Canopy to six months for Aquasana and Culligan and one year for the Sprite HO2, according to their makers and retailers. Heavier use or chloramine shortens cartridge life.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does NSF/ANSI 177 mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the standard for shower filtration systems, covering reduction of free available chlorine. Certified means an accredited body verified the claim; tested to means testing was done to the method, often commissioned by the brand. It does not cover claims about skin, hair or heavy metals.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a shower filter lower my water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Slightly, in some cases. Filters add resistance, and many filtered showerheads use 1.8 gpm heads to meet state limits. A clogging cartridge also reduces flow, which is a sign to replace it or to look at sediment filtration for the whole house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a shower filter with a handheld shower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you install an inline filter on the shower arm before the hose, so all water to the handheld passes through it. Filtered showerheads replace the head itself, so they only work with handhelds designed for them.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

